## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to apply k-way merge algorithms to partially sorted data, a common scenario in data processing pipelines and external sorting. It emphasizes understanding how the number of runs (r) impacts both time complexity and memory usage, connecting theoretical concepts with practical implementation.

## Key Concepts

- **k-way merge**: Efficient merging of multiple sorted sequences
- **Run detection**: Identifying sorted segments in a single linear pass
- **Min-heap**: Priority queue for selecting minimum elements efficiently
- **Complexity analysis**: O(n log r) time, O(r) auxiliary space
- **Edge cases**: Empty input, duplicate elements, and single-run scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
class MinHeap {
  constructor() { 
    this.heap = []; 
  }

  size() { 
    return this.heap.length; 
  }

  push(item) {
    this.heap.push(item);
    this._heapifyUp(this.heap.length - 1);
  }

  pop() {
    if (this.heap.length === 0) return null;
    const min = this.heap[0];
    const last = this.heap.pop();
    if (this.heap.length > 0) {
      this.heap[0] = last;
      this._heapifyDown(0);
    }
    return min;
  }

  _heapifyUp(index) { /* implementation */ }
  _heapifyDown(index) { /* implementation */ }
}

function kWayMerge(arr) {
  // Edge case: empty input
  if (arr.length === 0) return [];

  // Detect runs
  const runs = [];
  let start = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= arr.length; i++) {
    if (i === arr.length || arr[i-1] > arr[i]) {
      runs.push({start, end: i-1, index: start});
      start = i;
    }
  }

  // Edge case: r=1 (already sorted)
  if (runs.length === 1) return arr.slice();

  // Initialize heap with first elements
  const heap = new MinHeap();
  const result = [];

  runs.forEach((run, i) => {
    heap.push({value: arr[run.index], runId: i});
  });

  // Merge process
  while (heap.size() > 0) {
    const {value, runId} = heap.pop();
    result.push(value);

    const run = runs[runId];
    if (run.index < run.end) {
      run.index++;
      heap.push({value: arr[run.index], runId});
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to model task dependencies as a DAG and compute an optimal parallel schedule using topological ordering and dynamic programming.

## Key Concepts

- **Topological sort**: Ordering nodes such that all prerequisites come before dependent tasks
- **DAG DP**: Computing the longest path in a directed acyclic graph
- **Cycle detection**: Using indegree counts to identify cycles (if not all nodes are processed)
- **Time complexity**: O(n + m) where n is nodes and m is edges

## Code Example

```javascript
function earliestFinish(n, edges, duration) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v] of edges) {
    adj[u].push(v);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  const finish = duration.slice();
  const q = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  }

  let processed = 0;

  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    processed++;

    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      finish[v] = Math.max(finish[v], finish[u] + duration[v]);
      indeg[v]--;
      if (indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }

  if (processed !== n) return -1;
  return Math.max(...finish);
}
```

## Key Takeaways

- Unlimited workers means tasks run in parallel when dependencies allow
- The finish time of a task is the maximum of (prerequisite finish + its own duration)
- The pipeline completes when the slowest path (critical path) finishes

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests adaptive sorting algorithms, merge logic, and the ability to reason about time complexity in practical, maintainable ways.

## Key Concepts

- **Adaptive sorting**: Using a pre-check to achieve O(n) best-case performance when the array is already sorted
- **Iterative bottom-up mergesort**: Avoiding recursion by iteratively merging sorted subarrays of increasing size
- **Stability and memory usage**: Understanding the trade-offs of O(n) auxiliary space for guaranteed stability

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortWithEarlyMerge(arr) {
  // Early return if already sorted
  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] < arr[i - 1]) break;
    if (i === arr.length - 1) return arr;
  }

  // Iterative bottom-up mergesort
  const aux = [...arr];
  let width = 1;

  while (width < arr.length) {
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i += width * 2) {
      const left = i;
      const mid = Math.min(i + width, arr.length);
      const right = Math.min(i + width * 2, arr.length);

      let p = left, q = mid, k = left;
      while (p < mid && q < right) {
        aux[k++] = arr[p] <= arr[q] ? arr[p++] : arr[q++];
      }
      while (p < mid) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
      while (q < right) aux[k++] = arr[q++];
    }

    [arr, aux] = [aux, arr];
    width *= 2;
  }

  return arr;
}
```

## Time Complexity Analysis

- **Best case (O(n))**: Array is already sorted; single linear scan confirms this
- **Worst case (O(n log n))**: Array is reverse sorted; full mergesort required
- **Space complexity**: O(n) for the auxiliary buffer

